1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,611
Can you change this square without stretching any of its four sides?

2
00:00:04,611 --> 00:00:08,273
Push one corner sideways, and you get a diamond shape.

3
00:00:08,273 --> 00:00:11,664
The sides stay the same length. The angles change.

4
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:17,986
Now try the same idea with a solid. These bars keep their lengths, and every face must

5
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:21,724
stay perfectly flat. But a flat face can change shape.

6
00:00:21,724 --> 00:00:23,385
It is not a rigid plate.

7
00:00:23,767 --> 00:00:28,171
Watch the top of this cube move sideways and slightly down.

8
00:00:28,171 --> 00:00:31,456
The upright bars tilt instead of stretching.

9
00:00:31,456 --> 00:00:37,279
Every face remains flat, so this is an allowed change of shape, called a flex.

10
00:00:37,667 --> 00:00:40,750
Which solids can do this, and which are locked?

11
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:45,210
We will explore a family of ninety two shapes called Johnson solids.

12
00:00:45,210 --> 00:00:49,408
The answer is forty seven locally rigid and forty five flexible.

13
00:00:49,408 --> 00:00:51,179
Here is why that is subtle.

14
00:00:51,567 --> 00:00:56,352
Meet our running example, the triangular cupola, also called J three.

15
00:00:56,352 --> 00:01:02,246
It has a triangle on top, a hexagon underneath, and a ring of three squares and three

16
00:01:02,246 --> 00:01:05,367
triangles. All fifteen edges have length one.

17
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:11,607
Those triangles look like braces. Before we move anything, pause and predict: will this

18
00:01:11,607 --> 00:01:17,380
shape lock, or wobble? Remember that the hexagon and squares must stay flat, but their

19
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:19,327
angles are allowed to change.

20
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:24,585
It wobbles. This animation uses the actual construction checked in the research.

21
00:01:24,585 --> 00:01:29,775
Every bar keeps its length. The squares become rhombuses, and the hexagon changes its

22
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:32,156
angles while staying in a single plane.

23
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:39,058
The yellow line joins two corners that are not connected by an edge.

24
00:01:39,058 --> 00:01:44,826
Its length changes. That proves we are changing the shape itself, rather than just turning

25
00:01:44,826 --> 00:01:46,556
the entire object in space.

26
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,367
The trick is to build each corner by adding two positions.

27
00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:55,541
Start at the origin, follow one blue arrow, then one yellow arrow.

28
00:01:55,541 --> 00:02:00,852
The endpoint is their sum. This is ordinary coordinate addition, drawn as a journey.

29
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:04,747
For this cupola, the blue positions form a triangle.

30
00:02:04,747 --> 00:02:09,883
The yellow positions form a tetrahedron, a solid with four triangular faces.

31
00:02:09,883 --> 00:02:13,060
The outer corners of their sum make our cupola.

32
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,641
The colours track which component supplies each edge.

33
00:02:17,033 --> 00:02:23,448
Keep the blue triangle fixed. Turn the yellow tetrahedron a little around this vertical

34
00:02:23,448 --> 00:02:29,715
axis. Then add the positions again. We have a recipe for every corner, at every small

35
00:02:29,715 --> 00:02:33,401
angle, rather than a collection of guessed frames.

36
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:37,627
A blue edge comes entirely from the unchanged triangle.

37
00:02:37,627 --> 00:02:41,732
A yellow edge comes entirely from the rotating tetrahedron.

38
00:02:41,732 --> 00:02:45,628
Turning a rigid object does not change its edge lengths.

39
00:02:45,628 --> 00:02:48,968
That explains why every bar survives the motion.

40
00:02:49,367 --> 00:02:52,375
But unchanged edges are only half the rules.

41
00:02:52,375 --> 00:02:57,230
Look straight down at the hexagon. Its sides alternate blue and yellow.

42
00:02:57,230 --> 00:03:03,111
Each colour supplies a closed triangle of edge directions, so turning one colour still

43
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:05,231
leaves a closed six-sided path.

44
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:09,848
The turn happens inside this plane, so the hexagon stays flat.

45
00:03:09,848 --> 00:03:16,084
At a five degree turn, its angles alternate between one hundred and fifteen and one hundred

46
00:03:16,084 --> 00:03:20,264
and twenty five degrees. Its six sides still have length one.

47
00:03:20,633 --> 00:03:24,338
Each square combines one direction from each component.

48
00:03:24,338 --> 00:03:29,524
Two directions span a plane, so its four corners remain a flat parallelogram.

49
00:03:29,524 --> 00:03:35,653
The triangular faces stay triangles. The certificate checks these requirements and confirms

50
00:03:35,653 --> 00:03:37,337
the motion is nontrivial.

51
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:43,903
Compare a square pyramid, J one. It has four unit bars around the base and four unit

52
00:03:43,903 --> 00:03:49,411
bars rising to the tip. Could its base do the same square-to-rhombus trick?

53
00:03:49,411 --> 00:03:52,349
Let us try the necessary geometry first.

54
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:56,283
The tip must be equally far from all four base corners.

55
00:03:56,283 --> 00:04:00,606
Its shadow on the base must therefore be equally far from them too.

56
00:04:00,606 --> 00:04:05,381
For a rhombus, that would put the shadow at the crossing of the diagonals.

57
00:04:05,767 --> 00:04:09,493
Call the half-diagonal lengths a and b, and the height h.

58
00:04:09,493 --> 00:04:15,311
Pythagoras gives a squared plus h squared equals one, and b squared plus h squared equals

59
00:04:15,311 --> 00:04:18,319
one. Subtract: a squared must equal b squared.

60
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:21,498
So the two diagonals must have equal length.

61
00:04:21,498 --> 00:04:24,233
A rhombus with equal diagonals is a square.

62
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:30,020
The attempted squash fails. This is a small example of rigidity, already covered by earlier

63
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:32,500
work, rather than a new discovery here.

64
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:36,901
For complicated solids, we start with a simpler question.

65
00:04:36,901 --> 00:04:39,709
Give every corner a tiny velocity arrow.

66
00:04:39,709 --> 00:04:45,816
Which arrows avoid changing any edge length or face flatness at the very first instant?

67
00:04:45,816 --> 00:04:47,852
This is the first-order test.

68
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:53,487
For one horizontal bar, pulling an end along the bar immediately changes its length.

69
00:04:53,487 --> 00:04:58,053
Moving that end straight sideways has zero initial rate of length change.

70
00:04:58,053 --> 00:05:02,681
The test notices the linear part of a change, before squared terms matter.

71
00:05:03,067 --> 00:05:07,106
All these first-instant rules become simultaneous linear equations.

72
00:05:07,106 --> 00:05:10,001
Sliding or turning the whole solid always works.

73
00:05:10,001 --> 00:05:14,583
In three dimensions, three slides and three turns give six ordinary motions.

74
00:05:14,583 --> 00:05:17,779
We remove those because they do not change the shape.

75
00:05:18,167 --> 00:05:21,953
For forty one Johnson solids, no other velocity survives.

76
00:05:21,953 --> 00:05:27,865
That proves local rigidity. But an extra velocity arrow does not yet prove a real wobble.

77
00:05:27,865 --> 00:05:32,183
An apparent escape can disappear when you look at the next order.

78
00:05:32,567 --> 00:05:38,433
Here is a simpler model of that trap. Two unit bars lie in a straight line between fixed

79
00:05:38,433 --> 00:05:44,033
endpoints, two units apart. The middle joint seems free to move upwards at the first

80
00:05:44,033 --> 00:05:46,367
instant. Try to give it a height h.

81
00:05:46,767 --> 00:05:51,959
By Pythagoras, each bar would then have squared length one plus h squared.

82
00:05:51,959 --> 00:05:55,889
Keeping its length one requires h squared to equal zero.

83
00:05:55,889 --> 00:06:02,064
So the joint cannot actually rise. The red drawing is a rejected attempt, not an allowed

84
00:06:02,064 --> 00:06:02,415
flex.

85
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:08,204
The six exceptional Johnson solids have this kind of first-order false hope, although

86
00:06:08,204 --> 00:06:13,545
their equations are more complicated. Exact second-order certificates rule out every

87
00:06:13,545 --> 00:06:18,568
nonzero candidate direction. The only real solution left is no change of shape.

88
00:06:18,967 --> 00:06:25,122
Those six are J fifty eight, fifty nine, sixty, eighty one, eighty two and eighty three.

89
00:06:25,122 --> 00:06:31,418
For J sixty and J eighty two, one suitable stress pattern blocks all candidate directions.

90
00:06:31,418 --> 00:06:35,335
The other four are rigid without that stronger property.

91
00:06:35,733 --> 00:06:38,507
Now put all ninety two solids on one board.

92
00:06:38,507 --> 00:06:43,345
They begin with regular polygon faces and are convex, with no inward dents.

93
00:06:43,345 --> 00:06:49,022
The Johnson family excludes the familiar Platonic and Archimedean solids, and the prisms

94
00:06:49,022 --> 00:06:49,989
and antiprisms.

95
00:06:50,367 --> 00:06:53,407
Forty one are rigid by the first-order test.

96
00:06:53,407 --> 00:06:56,239
Forty five have actual continuous flexes.

97
00:06:56,239 --> 00:07:02,872
Thirty eight use a certified rotation of a component; seven use an augmented prism construction.

98
00:07:02,872 --> 00:07:06,879
The remaining six are locked by second-order obstructions.

99
00:07:07,267 --> 00:07:13,955
So forty one plus six gives forty seven locally rigid solids, alongside forty five flexible

100
00:07:13,955 --> 00:07:20,496
ones. Notice the distinction: fifty one have extra first-order directions, but only forty

101
00:07:20,496 --> 00:07:24,979
five really flex. A tangent direction is not a motion family.

102
00:07:25,367 --> 00:07:29,167
This is not a computer shaking shapes until something happens.

103
00:07:29,167 --> 00:07:34,133
Coordinates and certificates use exact algebraic numbers, including square roots.

104
00:07:34,133 --> 00:07:39,712
The first-order results are checked in two different formulations, using modular arithmetic

105
00:07:39,712 --> 00:07:41,735
and rigorously bounded intervals.

106
00:07:42,133 --> 00:07:47,344
For the flexible shapes, the evidence is a formula for an actual motion, together with

107
00:07:47,344 --> 00:07:50,495
checks of lengths, flatness and a changing distance.

108
00:07:50,495 --> 00:07:55,099
For the six locked shapes, exact algebra rules out the candidate directions.

109
00:07:55,099 --> 00:07:58,189
Written arguments connect those checks to rigidity.

110
00:07:58,567 --> 00:08:02,682
The research package reports a successful seventeen-stage replay.

111
00:08:02,682 --> 00:08:08,126
That is strong computational evidence with explicit assumptions, not a proof assistant

112
00:08:08,126 --> 00:08:10,532
formalisation or external peer review.

113
00:08:10,532 --> 00:08:16,103
This video checks its displayed cupola motion against the stored certificate; a separate

114
00:08:16,103 --> 00:08:18,319
coordinator audit remains separate.

115
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:21,412
Keep three ideas. Flat does not mean rigid.

116
00:08:21,412 --> 00:08:25,133
A recipe preserving every constraint proves a genuine flex.

117
00:08:25,133 --> 00:08:29,421
And a first-instant permission can still be blocked at second order.

118
00:08:29,421 --> 00:08:33,268
Together, those ideas explain the three colours in the table.

119
00:08:33,667 --> 00:08:37,560
Locally rigid means no small wobble from the starting shape.

120
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:43,530
We are not claiming there can never be a distant different shape with the same edge lengths.

121
00:08:43,530 --> 00:08:48,787
Nor do these constructions classify every possible motion of the flexible solids.

122
00:08:49,167 --> 00:08:54,943
The contribution is the complete Johnson table and its certificates, rather than a new

123
00:08:54,943 --> 00:09:00,585
general rigidity rule. The documented searches found no earlier full classification,

124
00:09:00,585 --> 00:09:05,958
but cannot prove priority. The paper and editable evidence accompany this video.

125
00:09:05,958 --> 00:09:08,175
Narration uses a synthetic voice.
